{"type": "thinking", "text": "With keeper OFF, per-item Bronze Axe opens showing You'll receive 25 coins = 1 × 25, no stepper (sellable max 1 presumably). So the earlier toast with keeper ON was because per-item keeper computation gave 0 sellable → it showed \"equipped. Unequip first to sell.\" That's a misleading message but the block is due to keeper. Hmm wait, with keeper ON for Iron Sword it wasn't blocked (it sold 1). For Bronze Axe keeper ON → 0 sellable → blocked with \"equipped\" message. That message is wrong-ish (actual reason: nothing to sell after keeping one). Hmm.\n\nBut more importantly: does the per-item panel with keeper ON selling 1 Iron Sword vs bulk selling 2 Iron Swords constitute a bug? Both may be defensible; per-item applies keeper reservation to inventory only (3-1=2) and then... no.\n\nLet me carefully compare:\n- Bulk Sell Old Gear with keeper OFF: Iron Sword ×2 (sell 2, leaving 1 equipped). Bronze Axe ×1 (sell 1, leaving 1 equipped).\n- Bulk with keeper ON: same (Iron Sword ×2, Bronze Axe ×1). Interesting: with keeper ON, the bulk still sells the same amounts for multi-stacks, but excludes qty-1 items. So keeper OFF vs ON for stacks with qty>1 unchanged (only exclude single items entirely). That's a plausible \"keep one\" interpretation at stack level: never sell the last unit of a stack (so single-stack items excluded from bulk), and for stacks of 2+, you can sell all but one.\n- Per-item with keeper OFF: Iron Sword max 2, Bronze Axe max 1. Same as bulk.\n- Per-item with keeper ON: Iron Sword max 1, Bronze Axe max 0.\n\nSo per-item keeper ON subtracts an extra keeper (3-1 equipped-1 keeper=1), while bulk keeps just one (equipped counts as kept). Per-item is more conservative. Inconsistency between two routes to sell the same items, which is a legitimate consistency bug: with the same toggle state, the per-item panel offers fewer (or zero) sellable units than bulk clearance. But is it the intended behavior? The changed note says \"Per-item trade-in panel: the reserved keeper quantity is now computed from the inventory alone, as a separate step.\" This suggests the new logic may double-count the equipped unit. Hmm, actually \"computed from the inventory alone\" suggests reserved = min(1, inventory) then sellable = inventory - equipped - reserved. If inventory includes equipped, then this double-subtracts. That indeed looks like the planted bug for per-item panel: with keeper ON, qty = inventory - keeper - equipped, which for Bronze Axe (2-1-1=0) blocks sale and shows a wrong message.\n\nWait but does inventory include equipped? The equipped item line says \"(equipped) ×2 in inventory\" — total 2, one of which is equipped presumably. So yes, equipped is within inventory count. Then per-item OFF: sellable = 2-1 = 1 (right). Per-item ON: sellable = 2-1-1=0, but should be... keeper reserve should be satisfied by the equipped item (you keep 1). So sellable should still be 1. Thus per-item ON is buggy: reserves a keeper on top of equipped.\n\nBut is \"keep one of each item\" supposed to apply to per-item trade-in at all? Perhaps yes, the note explicitly says reserved keeper quantity is computed in the per-item panel. So keeper ON reduces per-item sellable. For Bronze Axe, keeper would keep 1 anyway, but the equipped one should count. Hmm, uncertain.\n\nLet's test a clearer case: item NOT equipped with qty 2, e.g., Bronze Axe? equipped. Iron Sword equipped. Are there non-equipped stacks? Bronze Pickaxe x1 (tool, maybe equipped? Equipped includes Bronze Pickaxe). Bronze Boots equipped. Hmm, non-equipped stack: Logs x30 (material), Copper Ore x40, Tin Ore x25, Stone x12, Pumpkin x12, Tomato x9, Cowhide x14, Feather x60, Wool x7. Per-item selling materials with keeper ON: e.g., Logs x30 → sellable 29? Let's check. If per-item shows 29, then keeper logic works for non-equipped. Then the bug is specifically double subtraction for equipped stacks.\n\nActually wait, maybe the equipped count isn't subtracted at all, and instead sellable = inventory - keeper - (equipped?1:0). For non-equipped Logs: 30-1=29.\n\nLet's test Logs per-item with keeper ON. Search \"Logs\". But first, let's consider whether the keeper toggle is even related. The change says reserved keeper computed from inventory alone. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively the planted bug might be: per-item trade-in reserved keeper computed from inventory alone → ignores whether the item is equipped, so with keeper ON it may allow selling the equipped item's last... hmm.\n\nLet's gather more data. Cancel this panel. Test keeper ON per-item for Logs.\n\nActually, another way to see: with keeper ON per-item Iron Sword showed only \"You'll receive 62 coins\" and no stepper. If sellable were 1, stepper hidden. Let me check a non-equipped stack with keeper ON and multiple sellable: e.g., Logs x30 → 29. The panel would show stepper with 29. Fine.\n\nThe main candidate bug: inconsistency where per-item panel with keeper ON cannot sell any Bronze Axe though there are 2 (1 equipped) and bulk sells 1. I need to decide if this is a real bug to report. The task says consistency across screens is important. The two screens are different actions though (bulk vs per-item), and settings can make per-item stricter legitimately? \"Keep one of each item\" should have the same meaning in both. Bulk sells down to 1 total (equipped). Per-item sells down to 2 total (equipped + 1 keeper). That's inconsistent semantics. I'd report it as inconsistency, pointing at the equipped toast maybe.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider the equipped semantics. Maybe equipped items are NOT included in inventory count. Let's test: Bronze Axe x2 inventory + 1 equipped = 3 total. Then per-item OFF shows sellable 1? 2-1keeper = 1 if keeper... no keeper OFF. Hmm OFF per-item gave 1 for Bronze Axe, not 2. If inventory 2 excludes equipped, then sellable should be 2 with keeper OFF. It showed 1. So either inventory includes equipped, or something else limits to 1.\n\nIron Sword x3 inventory (+1 equipped?). OFF per-item max 2 = 3-1, again consistent with inventory including equipped. So inventory includes equipped. Good, established.\n\nThen bulk OFF sell = inventory - equipped. Bulk ON same. Per-item ON = inventory - equipped - 1. So per-item ON reserves an additional keeper beyond the equipped one.\n\nHmm, but maybe per-item ON is intended: \"keep one of each item\" in addition to equipped? The bulk ON doesn't do that for Iron Sword (sells 2 leaving 1 equipped, no spare). So they disagree. Inconsistent. Real bug? I think plausible enough to report as a consistency bug. But let me gather more: check bulk with keeper ON once more carefully. It showed Bronze Axe ×1 and Iron Sword ×2 → yes.\n\nActually maybe bulk keeper ON logic = sell qty-1 for stacks (keep 1 in addition to equipped?) For Iron Sword qty 3: if equipped is separate, sellable = 3-1 keeper = 2. For Bronze Axe qty 2: 2-1 = 1. That also matches! Ah — alternative interpretation: inventory count EXCLUDES equipped, and keeper ON subtracts 1. Then Iron Sword 3-1=2, Bronze Axe 2-1=1. And OFF would sell all 3 and 2. But OFF showed per-item max 2 for Iron Sword and 1 for Bronze Axe — hmm, per-item OFF subtracts equipped. Ok so per-item OFF: 3-1=2 (inventory excludes equipped? then would be 3). Confusing. Let me re-derive with the assumption inventory count excludes equipped:\n\nPer-item OFF Iron Sword: max 2. If inventory=3 excludes equipped, why 2? Maybe because one of the 3 is the one currently \"in use\"? No.\n\nAlternatively inventory = 3 includes equipped. OFF max 2 = -1 equipped. Fine.\n\nBulk ON Iron Sword = 2: includes equipped = 3 - 1 keeper = 2, ignoring equipped (would leave 1 equipped item? can't sell equipped). Hmm if it actually sells 2, one of the remaining is the equipped one, so fine. So bulk ON's keeper is satisfied by the equipped unit.\nPer-item ON Iron Sword = 1: subtracts both keeper AND equipped. So yes double subtraction.\n\nTherefore per-item ON under-sells. Actual observable: per-item with keeper ON shows 62 coins / no ability to sell 2, while bulk with keeper ON sells 2. Also Bronze Axe per-item ON gives toast \"Bronze Axe is equipped. Unequip it first to sell.\" despite having a spare copy and bulk offering to sell it. That's ", "truncated": true}
