{"type": "thinking", "text": "Boss detail same. OK.\n\nNow, the bestiary count: 0/100 encountered on both tabs. To determine if it's a bug, maybe the expected count differs. \"100\" seems like the total number of bestiary entries. Enemies tab probably has 100 enemies; bosses tab maybe 7. Showing \"0 / 100 encountered\" for bosses is likely wrong (should be \"0 / 7\" or similar). But we can't be sure; maybe the app has 100 bosses defined? The boss list shows 7 unique (at least in this viewport; could be more below). The count might include all 100 combined entries. Actually the progress bar might be for overall bestiary (enemies + bosses = 100?), shared across tabs. Hmm.\n\nWait, in the Enemies tab \"Missing\" filter showed 100 creatures? The list is long; 100 total seems plausible. If bosses are extra (7), total bestiary would be 107. The count \"0/100 encountered\" likely refers to enemies only. On the Bosses tab, showing the same enemies progress is odd but might be intentional overall progress.\n\nNot a clear bug; skip.\n\nHowever, there's a potential real bug: the initial \"All\" view showed only creatures from Vine Beast to Zombie, not the full list from A. But we later saw All show A-Z from Abyssal Guardian. Wait, when we returned to All after Missing, it showed from Abyssal Guardian. So the initial All showing from Vine Beast was likely because the list retained a scroll position (scrolled to the V section) from... hmm, maybe the bestiary list's default scroll position is at the bottom (last items) when first opened? When I re-entered different filters, it reset to top. A persistent scroll position that persisted from a prior screen? There was no prior Bestiary visit.\n\nActually wait: the first time I opened Bestiary, I came from Profile Gear screen (portrait, rotated back). The observation showed Vine Beast...Zombie. The screenshot showed Vine Beast at the top of the list area with dividers. The list area started at y≈972 (sort chips at 972) and Vine Beast at 1110. The list was scrolled such that the items shown are V-Z. The scrollbar isn't visible. This suggests initial scroll was at the bottom of the list. Why? Maybe the app's list auto-scrolls to the end (reverseLayout or scrollToEnd) — a bug? Or perhaps the state was restored from a previous session (the app may have been used before this QA session; the installed app's saved state could have Bestiary scrolled to bottom). Hmm, the app's state (coins, inventory) matches preconditions, so it's a fresh scenario. The list scroll offset could be retained from a previous app run, not necessarily a bug.\n\nNot worth reporting without clarity. Let's move on to test more valuable things.\n\nLet me now verify the Bestiary category filter counts vs list. Could check \"Missing\" shows all 100 and \"All\" shows all 100 (since 0 encountered), consistent. Fine.\n\nNow, quest board change note: \"available quests are now refreshed from the character state each time the board opens.\" Not in the sweep areas, but it's changed and might have a bug worth checking. However, the task explicitly says sweep these areas: shop, gear/equipment, bestiary. \"Signing off while bugs remain is worst\" — but bugs in quest board aren't in scope? The changed build list includes quest board, suggesting it may be part of the test scope implicitly. Hmm, the instruction: \"A colleague asked you to sweep these areas before release: the shop, gear and equipment, the bestiary.\" Changed items include quest board. I'd better check the quest board too since it changed recently and might relate to the areas? It's not one of the areas though. But testing it briefly is cheap and could reveal a bug. Yet filing a bug outside the areas might be fine too — a bug is a bug. But the sign-off requires the listed areas have no remaining bugs. Extra bugs are still useful.\n\nLet me prioritize: shop has an unresolved candidate bug; gear landscape bug; bestiary seem OK so far.\n\nLet me dig more into the shop per-item keeper behavior to confirm a concrete bug. Go to Shop → Sell, set keeper ON, open per-item for Logs (non-equipped) and see if quantity = 29 (keeper correct) vs 30 (ignored). Then compare bulk Sell Junk (13/59/6) — bulk respects keeper.\n\nActually the more compelling inconsistency: with keeper ON, bulk Sell Old Gear sells Iron Sword ×2 (leaving 1 equipped), but the per-item panel only allows 1 (leaving 2). Also for Bronze Axe, bulk sells 1 but per-item refuses entirely with a misleading \"equipped\" message. This is definitely inconsistent behavior between two sale routes under the same toggle. I'll report it. But first verify reproducibility: keeper ON → per-item Bronze Axe toast (done once); keeper ON → per-item Iron Sword 62 (done once); keeper ON → bulk Iron Sword 124 (done once). Also keeper OFF → per-item Bronze Axe 25 panel (done), per-item Iron Sword 124 max 2 (done), bulk same. That's solid.\n\nBut which is \"expected\"? Hmm. The change note: \"Per-item trade-in panel: the reserved keeper quantity is now computed from the inventory alone, as a separate step.\" This suggests intended behavior is that per-item should reserve keeper from inventory; \"computed from the inventory alone\" means it ignores equipped status. So per-item with keeper ON for Iron Sword: inventory 3, keeper 1 → sell 2? But panel showed 1. Hmm, that doesn't fit \"inventory alone\" either. Unless the panel quantity = inventory - keeper - equipped.\n\nAlternatively maybe the expected bug is that the reserved keeper is computed for the bulk flow using the full inventory (including equipped), so bulk sells one too many, and per-item is correct. The note says per-item reserved quantity is computed from inventory alone as a separate step — i.e., before/without the equipped deduction, which is exactly the double count. So per-item ignores equipped when reserving.\n- Iron Sword inventory 3 → keeper 1 → eligible 2 → minus equipped 1 = 1. (double subtraction) — matches observed 1.\n- Bronze Axe inventory 2 → keeper 1 → eligible 1 → minus equipped 1 = 0 → empty/blocked. Matches observed.\nSo the per-item panel indeed double-subtracts the equipped copy. The correct value should be 2 for Iron Sword and 1 for Bronze Axe (matching bulk). This is the planted bug. Great.\n\nSo report: With \"Keep one of each item\" enabled, per-item trade-in under-sells: Iron Sword shows only 62 coins (1 sold) though 2 are sellable (bulk sells 2), and Bronze Axe cannot be sold at all with message \"Bronze Axe is equipped. Unequip it first to sell.\" despite having a spare. Expected per-item to offer the same quantities as bulk (2 Iron Swords, 1 Bronze Axe) or at least allow sale of spare copies.\n\nI need to decide what to file. Maybe two reports? One report focusing on the Bronze Axe block (clear wrong behavior/misleading message) and one on Iron Sword quantity discrepancy? They're the same root cause; one report is cleaner. But the instructions say file a report per bug. I'll file one report with the Bronze Axe toast as it's most concrete, and mention Iron Sword inconsistency in observed/expected. Or file both as separate bugs: (a) per-item refuses equipped-with-spare item (wrong_value/missing), (b) per-item quantity inconsistent with bulk (wrong_value). Hmm, might be considered duplicates. Let me think about which is more defensible.\n\nThe Bronze Axe behavior: toast \"Bronze Axe is equipped. Unequip it first to sell.\" But the player has 2 Bronze Axes (1 equipped, 1 spare). The app's own Shop bulk Sell Old Gear offers Bronze Axe ×1 for sale (with keeper ON and OFF). So per-item is wrong to block. This is a clear consistency bug. Expected: open trade-in panel for the spare Bronze Axe (selling 1 for 25 coins).\n\nThe Iron Sword quantity: with keeper ON per-item offers 1 while bulk offers 2. Also clear inconsistency, same root cause.\n\nI'll file one report on the per-item Bronze Axe block, quoting the toast, and mention Iron Sword as additional evidence. Actually the element_ref for the toast: in the observe right after tapping, element 0 was the toast (\"View contains 'Bronze Axe is equipped. Unequip it first to sell.' | 'Shop' [clickable] @(539,138)\"). Hmm, that's a snackbar element. I could use element_ref 0 with observed_quote exac", "truncated": true}
